{
  "book": "EXO",
  "chapter": 2,
  "resources": {
    "costumbres": {
      "c-2b8cf032333aa365": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-103",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "She took for him an ark of bulrushes, and daubed it with slime and with pitch.",
        "traduccion": {
          "resourceId": "freeman-103",
          "sourceHash": "6e34b8c6797ea8577a83b5b2c623ac8b527e73ddcb473ec97e2bc2883e8f9713",
          "sourceLanguage": "en"
        }
      },
      "c-5b68084f730b66cc": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-104",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "It would be quite inconsistent with modern Oriental ideas of propriety for women to bathe thus publicly; but among the ancient Egyptians it was admissible. Wilkinson (Anc. Egypt, vol. 3, p. 389) gives a picture from the monuments representing an Egyptian woman of rank bathing, at…",
        "traduccion": {
          "resourceId": "freeman-104",
          "sourceHash": "c2f31e11145426014bfa7182b5af44a2b15940309097728b750fad0deb3b3530",
          "sourceLanguage": "en"
        }
      },
      "c-a9e3ae269320912a": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-105",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "In general the proposal of marriage came from the family of the bridegroom; but occasionally this custom was reversed, as in the case referred to in the text. Caleb gave his daughter Achsah to Othniel (Josh. 15:16-17). Saul gave his daughter Michal to David (1 Sam. 18:27).",
        "traduccion": {
          "resourceId": "freeman-105",
          "sourceHash": "6b4efa4d667358eb9c245dfc456d22ac2be99596aa90444c0715d307efa1eb2a",
          "sourceLanguage": "en"
        }
      }
    },
